#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb008515-d668-509b-8c8a-76e892e709cc" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over a Part-Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecb64b3d-3f2f-5697-b997-2c7aa4be2cab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8∫_{3π/2}^{2π}\\cos te^{4\\sin t}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177395} {:answer/id #uuid "fd980ed9-3298-5862-ba07-20802d91a56b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16∫_{3π/2}^{2π}\\cos te^{4\\sin t}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177396} {:answer/id #uuid "01e7cc4c-0aeb-5b50-907f-d996e7ff5cb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16∫_{3π/2}^{2π}\\sin te^{4\\cos t}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177397} {:answer/id #uuid "4b52514f-27c7-52ee-92a9-a655b53b37c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8∫_{3π/2}^{2π}\\sin te^{4\\cos t}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177398} {:answer/id #uuid "5754a7c5-acfc-5e04-99f8-c1cb5e7513f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4∫_{π/2}^{2π}\\cos te^{4\\sin t}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd980ed9-3298-5862-ba07-20802d91a56b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16∫_{3π/2}^{2π}\\cos te^{4\\sin t}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177396} :answer-field/id #uuid "b259fcc0-d096-565c-8ce4-0a482d9249b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dcd58e0c-8630-5c7a-a6a1-6bb6a8bcb260" :question/math-academy-id "q-122440" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$ along the quarter-circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ traversed from the point $(0,-4)$ to the point $(4,0)$ in the fourth quadrant?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O,$ shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066920/assets/0baaafbe9846ccef2c06a3b1a86eccac394344afc2dfd94bba2907ebb1cf2362.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot 4\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353727 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c33b34e-5b57-51c1-91d0-e098364bcdfb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Function Over a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b540412-fb2a-5b6a-b348-c7da441e4824" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt[3]{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353729} {:answer/id #uuid "ee828b3e-e473-596e-85f7-3e0fd1af502e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353730} {:answer/id #uuid "5f974ac2-483f-5772-b9f5-eaf5c71f278e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353731} {:answer/id #uuid "ef53b03d-aeed-571f-be0a-8c3ae73d4c2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353732} {:answer/id #uuid "1b340d24-d37d-51e7-9982-4014c14fff51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353733}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b340d24-d37d-51e7-9982-4014c14fff51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353733} :answer-field/id #uuid "651a1703-0c2f-55f2-8943-f99e23f62f98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353728}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f7752cbf-08cf-5d87-a957-774e73db29fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-122576" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x\\sqrt[3]{y+1}$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $6$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{sin}^{2}⁡t+36{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x\\sqrt[3]{y+1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt[3]{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt[3]{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1}\\cdot 6\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt[3]{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1,$ $\\text{d}u=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt[3]{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =6{\\int }_{1}^{1}\\sqrt[3]{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353734 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb008515-d668-509b-8c8a-76e892e709cc" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over a Part-Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6720c71e-bf94-5157-992a-646387a565be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353736} {:answer/id #uuid "a9b7927a-d025-5449-bb92-0d1ae5714112" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353737} {:answer/id #uuid "ebae7482-f777-5b07-8446-e419b7aecf9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353738} {:answer/id #uuid "46afe35a-b76a-598a-a8a2-29051f66cb84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353739} {:answer/id #uuid "f8df536b-9828-55d1-a5d8-b2c815d0ff45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8df536b-9828-55d1-a5d8-b2c815d0ff45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353740} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed03dcbc-c76f-58e5-8b96-ed8d21754b75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7aa8bcd7-fed3-5186-b1c1-b187da2c7965" :question/math-academy-id "q-122586" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=y\\,\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}$ along the quarter-circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ traversed from the point $(0,2)$ to the point $(-2,0)$ in the second quadrant?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066920/assets/d0ae53dd5d0932cbf834a166e851c227f75cb546b591a9fd43930ec06f0e9ddf.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=y\\,\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\sqrt{4-(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\sqrt{4-4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }4{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353741 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb008515-d668-509b-8c8a-76e892e709cc" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over a Part-Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "815a9ce4-8a9e-52df-9203-3c47063c150f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353743} {:answer/id #uuid "fc45b25a-96e6-5c4f-882b-de3fbeefa221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353744} {:answer/id #uuid "203663f0-e4fa-5bee-a49a-710dab722a70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353745} {:answer/id #uuid "2bcd5417-c265-5c0e-940e-e49d451bafbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353746} {:answer/id #uuid "883b6f90-4e44-5d00-928e-f2a8afcabe13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353747}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "883b6f90-4e44-5d00-928e-f2a8afcabe13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353747} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f222c2d-1d67-5b8b-8f80-a3ef50cb0148" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a882cfb-b3f3-519e-9b6e-6e0eebe95df2" :question/math-academy-id "q-122591" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}$ along the quarter-circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ traversed from the point $(-2,0)$ to the point $(0,-2)$ in the third quadrant?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066920/assets/1fd6c2df8b8467a46ed36b515842b88656883733376ee732839bab4085ca019c.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =8{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}8{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353748 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c33b34e-5b57-51c1-91d0-e098364bcdfb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Function Over a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84e27d12-f336-52f9-baff-901d2d2eeae9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353750} {:answer/id #uuid "af6af82e-9fda-5e70-9681-c906c95b7d2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353751} {:answer/id #uuid "a5e14576-b4c1-58c9-96bb-49e903faea0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353752} {:answer/id #uuid "fe161fc8-cc44-59ef-80d4-36624be86331" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353753} {:answer/id #uuid "e42e4650-7663-5d04-aa21-52c2c1478455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5e14576-b4c1-58c9-96bb-49e903faea0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353752} :answer-field/id #uuid "83ec200b-d80e-59da-8330-6e370e4d9b76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2c494e76-b8ff-570a-84f4-aa9af6bfbb6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-122594" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=2x-3$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $3$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2x-3,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-3 \\\\  & =3(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }3(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\cdot 3\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\,\\text{d}t & =9[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =9[2(0-0)-(2\\pi -0)] \\\\  & =-18\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
